# Write a PRD

**Goal**: a spec so precise that "done" can be proven by running the system.

## Steps
1. `./scripts/sdlc new <slug>` (skip if the folder exists). Open `.ai/specs/FEAT-xxx-<slug>/prd.md`.
2. Restate the request in one or two sentences under **Objective**. If you cannot, ask the requester; do not guess.
3. Enumerate behaviours as acceptance criteria, one line each: `- **AC-001** Given ..., when ..., then ...`.
   - Happy path first, then each validation rule, not-found, unauthorized, empty and boundary case.
   - Observable from outside (HTTP status/body, page content, file/command result). No class or table names.
4. Fill **Permissions**, **Data & validation**, **Contract impact**, **Out of scope**. Anything you assumed goes into an "Open questions" list at the end instead of being silently decided.
5. Write `acceptance.json`: for every AC at least one check (see the write-fact-checks skill). Prefer `http` against the running system.
6. Delete every `TODO(prd)` marker. Run `./scripts/sdlc facts --feature FEAT-xxx --runtime none`: traceability must pass (checks needing the runtime are skipped at this point; that is fine).

## Exit criteria
- Every AC has >= 1 check, every check cites an AC.
- A reviewer who never saw the request can tell exactly what to build and how it will be verified.

## Pitfalls
- Vague words ("fast", "user-friendly", "handles errors"): replace with numbers and status codes.
- Criteria that restate the implementation. Describe behaviour, not design.
- Forgetting the unhappy paths: that is where most bugs live.

Reference: `.ai/rules/prd.md`, worked example `.ai/specs/FEAT-000-hello-world/`.
